# oomd — the out-of-memory policy service.
#
# When memmgr's pressure level has read CRITICAL for OOMD_GRACE_MS, oomd stops
# one candidate of its stop sources, at most one per OOMD_COOLDOWN_MS: a
# program a `stop_source` service lists before any service, and within that
# the one using the most memory (services/oomd/docs/operation.md). svcmgr
# restarts a stopped service under its restart policy. A service is stoppable
# only when its recipe sets `oom_stop = yes` and svcmgr's eligibility rule
# admits it; no recipe of the default image sets `oom_stop` or `stop_source`,
# so by default oomd stops nothing. `OOMD_STOP=no` makes oomd name the
# candidate it would stop and stop none.
# Removing this recipe from the image removes the policy: memory then runs out
# for whichever process asks next, and the reserve keeps the core alive.
#
# `stop_authority = yes` makes svcmgr mint a SEND on its own endpoint badged
# with STOP_AUTHORITY and deliver it in the bootstrap slot after the seeds
# and the debug-authority cap (cap[0] here, with neither): oomd lists and
# stops services through it, learns the other stop sources through it, and
# holds no process handle.
#
# `reserve = yes` makes oomd's budget node a system-reserve node, so it runs
# while the free pool outside the reserve is spent; it touches its stack and
# heap at start and allocates nothing after. A holder of either key has a core
# role: no stop-authority holder may stop oomd.
#
# `restart = on_failure`, `critical = no`: without oomd the system runs, with
# no policy. crashd's recipe holds a fault declined for want of memory for
# longer than the grace and the cooldown together, so a fault held while oomd
# stops a service resumes.
binary          = /services/oomd
priority        = 20
restart         = on_failure
critical        = no
env             = OOMD_GRACE_MS=2000 OOMD_COOLDOWN_MS=5000 OOMD_STOP=yes
namespace       = none
stop_authority  = yes
reserve         = yes
